###Pure
// product-pricing -- how each kind of product is turned into a number.
//
// Layer 2. It depends on product-core (the taxonomy of what the firm sells) and on
// valuation-core (what a position is worth, and the arithmetic that carries it into the
// reporting currency). It refers to nothing else.
//
// The thesis of this project is that THE PRODUCT TYPE PICKS THE MODEL, and the model picks
// everything after it -- the inputs it needs, the calibration behind those inputs, the
// greeks it can produce, and the reserve held because it is only a model:
//
//    vanilla, linear payoff          a closed form. One evaluation, exact greeks.
//    path dependent (autocall, ...)  Monte Carlo. A path count, a seed, a standard error.
//    early exercise (American, ...)  a lattice. A step count, and a backward induction.
//    deposits and financing          accrual and discounting. No market model at all.
//    funds                           the administrator's NAV. Not a model, a report.
//    everything nobody trusts        a broker mark and a reserve big enough to admit it.
//
// So the class hierarchy is rooted in the DEPENDENCY's taxonomy rather than in a new one:
// product_pricing::PprPricedProduct extends product_core::Product, and its mapping set
// extends product-core's [prdProduct], which itself extends core-instrument's [ciBase].
// A priced product is a product is an instrument, three projects deep, on one row.
//
// Two classes go further down the taxonomy and extend a CONCRETE product type --
// PprPricedAutocall extends product_core::YieldEnhancementProduct, PprPricedProtectedNote
// extends product_core::CapitalProtectedNote -- because the pricing facts that matter for
// those two are facts about that payoff and nothing else. Their properties are mapped on
// their OWN sets: a property declared on a subclass cannot be mapped on the parent's set,
// which is the trap product-core hit and recorded.
//
// The second theme is the CROSS-PROJECT FUNCTION CALL, inherited from valuation-core's own
// reason for existing. This project does not re-derive a dirty price or a market value; it
// calls valuation_core::vcDirtyPrice and valuation_core::vcMarketValue, which in turn call
// core-fx. The model price and the market price therefore travel through exactly the same
// arithmetic, which is the only way a model-versus-market deviation means anything.
//
// Prefixes per the project contract: classes Ppr*, tables PPR_*, joins Ppr_*, filters Ppr*,
// set ids ppr*, functions ppr*.

// ============================================================ the priced product taxonomy

// A catalogued product the pricing desk has taken responsibility for: it has a model, a
// desk, a currency it is priced in, and a reserve rate held against the model being wrong.
// The root of this project's overlay, and the point where it joins the dependency's
// taxonomy -- everything product-core knows about the product is inherited, not repeated.
Class product_pricing::PprPricedProduct extends product_core::Product
{
   // The PprPricingModel that prices this product. The single fact this whole project is
   // organised around: one product, one model, and an audit trail for the choice.
   modelCode: String[1];
   // ANALYTIC, MONTE_CARLO, LATTICE, ACCRUAL, NAV or BROKER. Denormalised from the model so
   // a report can group by technique without walking to PPR_MODEL, and it is the
   // discriminator the six subtype sets below filter on.
   modelFamily: String[1];
   // IN_MODEL or OUT_OF_SCOPE. A product can be catalogued and still not be priced here --
   // a fund distributed but not manufactured, for instance.
   pricingStatus: String[1];
   // ISO 4217 the model produces its number in. Not necessarily the settlement currency.
   pricingCurrency: String[0..1];
   // The desk that owns the mark. Whoever this is, is who explains a break.
   pricingDesk: String[0..1];
   // valuation-core's VcValuationMethod code (MARK, MODEL, BROKER), so the tolerance this
   // product is held to is the one the firm's valuation policy already states.
   valuationMethodCode: String[0..1];
   // The discount curve the model discounts on. Every model here needs one; only some need
   // a volatility surface, which is why that lives on the subtypes.
   discountCurveCode: String[0..1];
   // The volatility surface the model reads, when it reads one at all.
   volatilitySurfaceCode: String[0..1];
   // When the model last produced a price for this product.
   lastPricedOn: StrictDate[0..1];
   // Model risk tier, 1 (material) to 3 (immaterial). Drives review frequency and reserve.
   modelTier: Integer[0..1];
   // True once model validation has signed the model off FOR THIS PRODUCT. A validated
   // model used outside its validated scope is an unvalidated model.
   isModelValidated: Boolean[1];
   // DAILY, WEEKLY or MONTHLY -- how often the number is expected to move.
   revaluationFrequency: String[0..1];
   // True when an independent price verification team re-marks it separately.
   hasIndependentPriceCheck: Boolean[1];
   // The standing model reserve, in basis points of model value, before any product-specific
   // uncertainty is added on top.
   baseReserveBasisPoints: Float[0..1];

   // The model itself, with its engine, its version and its validation history.
   model: product_pricing::PprPricingModel[0..1];
   // The curve this product discounts on.
   discountCurve: product_pricing::PprDiscountCurve[0..1];
   // Every price this product has been given, across runs.
   prices: product_pricing::PprProductPrice[*];

   // The standing reserve as a rate rather than basis points, so two projects quoting the
   // same reserve agree on the scale. `/` widens to Float, hence Float[1].
   baseReserveRate() { $this.baseReserveBasisPoints->toOne() / 10000.0 } : Float[1];
   // Whether this is a tier-one model risk, which is the question a model risk committee
   // agenda is actually sorted on.
   isTierOne() { $this.modelTier->toOne() == 1 } : Boolean[1];
   // Whether the number may be reported without a caveat: validated, and independently
   // checked. Anything else goes to the committee with a reserve attached.
   isTrustedMark() { $this.isModelValidated && $this.hasIndependentPriceCheck } : Boolean[1];
}

// A product priced by a closed-form formula -- one evaluation, no simulation, no lattice.
// What distinguishes it: the greeks come out of the same formula as the price, so they are
// exact rather than bumped, and the only real modelling choice left is the volatility point.
Class product_pricing::PprAnalyticPricedProduct extends product_pricing::PprPricedProduct
{
   // BLACK_SCHOLES, BLACK_76, BACHELIER, GARMAN_KOHLHAGEN -- which closed form.
   closedFormName: String[0..1];
   // True when the formula is fed a strike-dependent volatility rather than a single ATM
   // number. The difference between the two is most of the mis-pricing in this family.
   usesVolatilitySmile: Boolean[1];
   // The single implied volatility the formula is fed, when it is fed one.
   quotedVolatility: Float[0..1];
   // ACT/365, ACT/360 or 30/360 -- how the formula measures time to expiry.
   analyticDayCount: String[0..1];
   // True when the same formula also returns the greeks, which is the point of using it.
   hasAnalyticGreeks: Boolean[1];
}

// A product priced by simulating paths, because its payoff depends on the whole path and
// not only on where the underlying finishes. What distinguishes it: the answer is an
// estimate with a standard error, so the path count is a price of accuracy and the seed is
// part of the audit trail.
Class product_pricing::PprMonteCarloPricedProduct extends product_pricing::PprPricedProduct
{
   // How many paths the production run uses. Halving the error costs four times this.
   pathCount: Integer[0..1];
   // Time steps per year on each path. Discretisation error hides here, not in pathCount.
   timeStepsPerYear: Integer[0..1];
   // The seed, recorded so a price can be reproduced exactly. Without it the number is not
   // reproducible and therefore not auditable.
   randomSeed: Integer[0..1];
   // ANTITHETIC, CONTROL_VARIATE, SOBOL or NONE.
   varianceReduction: String[0..1];
   // The standard deviation across paths, which is what the standard error is computed from.
   pathStandardDeviation: Float[0..1];
   // True when the payoff genuinely reads the path -- a barrier, an average, an autocall.
   // False here means the product is being simulated when a formula would do.
   isPathDependent: Boolean[1];

   // The Monte Carlo standard error: the honest error bar on the price.
   standardError() { product_pricing::pprMonteCarloStandardError($this.pathStandardDeviation->toOne(), $this.pathCount->toOne()) } : Float[1];
   // Total simulated steps per path count -- the cost of the run, in one integer.
   totalTimeSteps() { $this.pathCount->toOne() * $this.timeStepsPerYear->toOne() } : Integer[1];
}

// A product priced on a lattice, because the holder can exercise before expiry and the
// value therefore has to be compared with the exercise value at every node. What
// distinguishes it: backward induction, so the step count is the accuracy, and discrete
// dividends can be placed on actual dates rather than smeared into a yield.
Class product_pricing::PprLatticePricedProduct extends product_pricing::PprPricedProduct
{
   // BINOMIAL, TRINOMIAL or PDE_GRID.
   latticeType: String[0..1];
   // Steps from today to expiry. Too few and the early-exercise boundary is a staircase.
   treeSteps: Integer[0..1];
   // AMERICAN or BERMUDAN. European on a tree is a formula wearing a costume.
   exerciseStyle: String[0..1];
   // Time to expiry in years, which with treeSteps gives the step size.
   yearsToExpiry: Float[0..1];
   // True when discrete dividends sit on their ex-dates rather than being turned into a
   // continuous yield -- which is exactly what decides early exercise on a call.
   handlesDiscreteDividends: Boolean[1];

   // The size of one step, in years. The number a convergence test is stated against.
   stepSizeYears() { product_pricing::pprLatticeStepSize($this.yearsToExpiry->toOne(), $this.treeSteps->toOne()) } : Float[1];
}

// A product priced by accruing and discounting contractual cash flows: deposits, repo,
// margin loans. What distinguishes it: there is no market model and no volatility -- the
// only inputs are a rate, a day count and a discount curve, and the only judgement is
// whether the firm's own funding curve or a collateralised one is the right one.
Class product_pricing::PprAccrualPricedProduct extends product_pricing::PprPricedProduct
{
   // ACT/360, ACT/365 or 30/360 -- how the contract counts days, not how the model does.
   accrualBasis: String[0..1];
   // The contractual rate, as a percentage. Fixed for the life of most of this family.
   contractRatePercent: Float[0..1];
   // True when the position is carried at fair value rather than at amortised cost. The
   // choice is an accounting election and it changes what this class produces.
   isFairValueOption: Boolean[1];
   // Days from the valuation date to the contractual end.
   daysToMaturity: Integer[0..1];
   // The spread over the discount curve applied for the firm's own credit.
   fundingSpreadBasisPoints: Float[0..1];
}

// A product priced at the administrator's published net asset value. What distinguishes it:
// this is not a model at all, it is a REPORT, and everything difficult about it is
// operational -- how old the NAV is, whether it is estimated, and what swing pricing did.
Class product_pricing::PprNavPricedProduct extends product_pricing::PprPricedProduct
{
   // The administrator or vendor the NAV is taken from.
   navSource: String[0..1];
   // How many business days behind the valuation date the NAV is. Non-zero for most funds
   // of funds, and the single largest source of stale marks in this family.
   navLagDays: Integer[0..1];
   // The swing applied by the fund to protect remaining holders, as a percentage of NAV.
   swingFactorPercent: Float[0..1];
   // True when the NAV used is the administrator's estimate rather than the final one.
   usesEstimatedNav: Boolean[1];
   // The published NAV per unit actually used.
   navPerUnit: Float[0..1];
}

// A product carried at a broker's quote because nobody has a model they are willing to
// defend. What distinguishes it: the number is somebody else's opinion, so the facts that
// matter are how many opinions there were and how far apart they were -- and the reserve
// is sized from that dispersion rather than from any model diagnostic.
Class product_pricing::PprBrokerMarkedProduct extends product_pricing::PprPricedProduct
{
   // How many independent brokers quoted. One is a rumour; three is a mark.
   brokerCount: Integer[0..1];
   // The spread between the highest and lowest quote, as a percentage of the mid. This
   // number, not a model diagnostic, is what sizes the reserve for this family.
   brokerDispersionPercent: Float[0..1];
   // The uncertainty reserve held on top of the standing reserve, in basis points.
   uncertaintyReserveBasisPoints: Float[0..1];
   // Who has to explain the mark when it is challenged. A named person, not a team.
   escalationOwner: String[0..1];
   // True when the desk that trades the product also sourced the quotes, which is the
   // control weakness this class exists to make visible.
   isDeskSourced: Boolean[1];

   // The total reserve rate for a broker mark: the standing model reserve plus the
   // uncertainty the dispersion justifies.
   totalReserveRate() { ($this.baseReserveBasisPoints->toOne() + $this.uncertaintyReserveBasisPoints->toOne()) / 10000.0 } : Float[1];
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------- two concrete payoffs, priced

// The pricing view of an autocallable yield-enhancement note -- extends the DEPENDENCY's
// concrete product class, not this project's root. What distinguishes it: the note's life
// is itself a random variable, so an expected life and an autocall probability are
// simulation OUTPUTS that have to be stored beside the price to explain it.
Class product_pricing::PprPricedAutocall extends product_core::YieldEnhancementProduct
{
   // How many autocall observation dates remain.
   observationCount: Integer[0..1];
   // QUARTERLY, SEMI_ANNUAL or ANNUAL -- the observation schedule.
   observationFrequency: String[0..1];
   // True when missed coupons are paid retrospectively once a coupon barrier is regained.
   // Memory turns a simple simulation into a path-state one.
   hasMemoryCoupon: Boolean[1];
   // The simulated probability of being called at the next observation date.
   autocallProbability: Float[0..1];
   // Expected life in years under the model. The number a sales desk quotes as "the term"
   // and the number a treasury desk funds against; they are the same number and it is here.
   expectedLifeYears: Float[0..1];
   // The simulated probability of ending below the downside barrier at maturity -- the
   // client's actual chance of losing principal, which the brochure states as a barrier.
   barrierBreachProbability: Float[0..1];
   // The model that produced those probabilities. Path dependent, so always Monte Carlo.
   pricingModelCode: String[0..1];

   // Expected life spread over the observations still to come -- how long, on average, the
   // note is expected to survive per remaining chance to be called. `->toFloat()` on the
   // count because Integer against Float would type as Number.
   yearsPerRemainingObservation() { $this.expectedLifeYears->toOne() / $this.observationCount->toOne()->toFloat() } : Float[1];
}

// The pricing view of a capital-protected note -- extends the dependency's concrete class.
// What distinguishes it: the note is priced as TWO instruments, a zero-coupon bond for the
// protection and an option for the upside, so the two present values are carried
// separately and their sum is the check that the decomposition is complete.
Class product_pricing::PprPricedProtectedNote extends product_core::CapitalProtectedNote
{
   // Present value of the zero-coupon leg, as a percentage of face. This is the protection,
   // and it is worth less the higher rates are -- which is why these notes come and go.
   zeroLegPresentValuePercent: Float[0..1];
   // Present value of the option leg, as a percentage of face. Whatever the zero leg left.
   optionLegPresentValuePercent: Float[0..1];
   // The issuer funding spread used to discount the zero leg. The protection is only as
   // good as the issuer, and this basis-point number is where that is priced.
   issuerFundingSpreadBasisPoints: Float[0..1];
   // True when the protection is modelled rather than assumed -- conditional protection is
   // a barrier option and must be simulated, not discounted.
   isProtectionModelled: Boolean[1];
   // The model that priced the option leg.
   pricingModelCode: String[0..1];

   // The two legs added back up, as a percentage of face. Materially away from 100 and the
   // decomposition is missing a fee, a spread or a leg.
   decompositionCheckPercent() { $this.zeroLegPresentValuePercent->toOne() + $this.optionLegPresentValuePercent->toOne() } : Float[1];
}

// ============================================================ the models themselves

// A pricing model as an installed, versioned, owned thing: an engine, a version, a quant
// team and an approval status. The root of the model hierarchy, and what a product points
// at when it says "this is how I am priced".
Class product_pricing::PprPricingModel
{
   // The model identifier carried on every product and every price it produced.
   modelCode: String[1];
   // What the model is called in the model inventory.
   modelName: String[1];
   // ANALYTIC, MONTE_CARLO or LATTICE. The discriminator for the three subtypes below.
   modelKind: String[1];
   // The library the implementation lives in.
   engineName: String[0..1];
   // The version of it. A price is only reproducible against a version, so this is part of
   // the identity of every number this project stores.
   engineVersion: String[0..1];
   // What the model is approved to price: EQUITY, RATES, FX, CREDIT, HYBRID.
   assetClass: String[0..1];
   // valuation-core's method code, so the model inherits that project's tolerance.
   valuationMethodCode: String[0..1];
   // The quant team that owns the implementation and answers for it.
   owningQuantTeam: String[0..1];
   // APPROVED, CONDITIONAL, RESTRICTED or WITHDRAWN.
   approvalStatus: String[0..1];
   // True when it is the model production actually runs, as opposed to a research build.
   isProduction: Boolean[1];
   // Typical single-price runtime in milliseconds. A model too slow to run overnight is a
   // model that will be silently replaced by a spreadsheet.
   typicalRuntimeMillis: Integer[0..1];
   // Model risk tier, 1 to 3.
   modelRiskTier: Integer[0..1];

   // valuation-core's definition of the method this model reports under.
   valuationMethod: valuation_core::VcValuationMethod[0..1];
   // The inputs it requires before it will produce a number.
   inputs: product_pricing::PprModelInput[*];
   // Which product subtypes it has been assigned to price.
   assignments: product_pricing::PprModelAssignment[*];
   // Its calibration history.
   calibrations: product_pricing::PprCalibration[*];
   // Its validation history.
   validations: product_pricing::PprModelValidation[*];
   // The limitations validation recorded against it.
   limitations: product_pricing::PprModelLimitation[*];

   // Whether a price from this model may be reported without a caveat.
   isApprovedForProduction() { $this.isProduction && ($this.approvalStatus->toOne() == 'APPROVED') } : Boolean[1];
}

// A model with a closed-form solution: the price and its greeks come out of one formula.
// What distinguishes it: nothing about it is approximate except the assumptions, so the
// only modelling decision left is how the volatility smile is fed to a formula that
// assumes there is not one.
Class product_pricing::PprAnalyticModel extends product_pricing::PprPricingModel
{
   // BLACK_SCHOLES, BLACK_76, BACHELIER, GARMAN_KOHLHAGEN.
   formulaName: String[0..1];
   // True when the formula returns greeks analytically rather than by bumping.
   hasClosedFormGreeks: Boolean[1];
   // STICKY_STRIKE, STICKY_DELTA or NONE -- how the smile is handled by a formula that
   // does not have one. The choice moves the delta, not the price.
   smileTreatment: String[0..1];
   // The upper bound of moneyness the formula is validated over. Outside it the number is
   // an extrapolation, whatever the engine reports.
   validatedMoneynessMax: Float[0..1];
}

// A model that prices by simulation. What distinguishes it: the answer has an error bar,
// so the settings that control that error bar -- paths, sequence, variance reduction --
// are model configuration and not run-time preference.
Class product_pricing::PprMonteCarloModel extends product_pricing::PprPricingModel
{
   // The production path count, before any product-specific override.
   defaultPathCount: Integer[0..1];
   // True when antithetic sampling is available: the cheapest variance reduction there is.
   supportsAntithetic: Boolean[1];
   // True when the engine can draw a low-discrepancy sequence instead of pseudo-random.
   supportsQuasiRandom: Boolean[1];
   // True when a Brownian bridge is used to place barrier monitoring between time steps --
   // without it, a discretely monitored barrier is systematically under-triggered.
   usesBrownianBridge: Boolean[1];
   // The convergence target, in basis points of price, the run is expected to reach.
   convergenceTargetBasisPoints: Float[0..1];
}

// A model that prices by backward induction on a lattice. What distinguishes it: it can
// answer "is it worth exercising here", which no closed form and no forward simulation can
// do without extra machinery, and that is the only reason to accept its cost.
Class product_pricing::PprLatticeModel extends product_pricing::PprPricingModel
{
   // BINOMIAL, TRINOMIAL or PDE_GRID.
   latticeType: String[0..1];
   // The default number of steps to expiry.
   defaultStepCount: Integer[0..1];
   // True when continuous early exercise is supported.
   supportsAmerican: Boolean[1];
   // True when exercise is supported on a stated schedule rather than continuously.
   supportsBermudan: Boolean[1];
   // ESCROWED, SPOT_ADJUSTED or CONTINUOUS_YIELD -- how discrete dividends are placed.
   dividendTreatment: String[0..1];
}

// The decision that a given product subtype is priced by a given model, with who decided
// and why. Separate from the product because the assignment is a POLICY that applies to a
// class of products, and it is the artefact a model risk review actually reads.
Class product_pricing::PprModelAssignment
{
   // The product-core INSTRUMENT_SUBTYPE the assignment applies to, e.g. YIELD_ENHANCEMENT.
   productSubtype: String[1];
   // The model assigned to it.
   modelCode: String[1];
   // When the assignment took effect.
   assignedOn: StrictDate[0..1];
   // Who signed it.
   assignedBy: String[0..1];
   // True for the model actually used; false rows are the documented alternatives.
   isPrimary: Boolean[1];
   // What is used when the primary model fails to converge or is withdrawn.
   fallbackModelCode: String[0..1];
   // Why this model and not another. The one field a reviewer reads first.
   rationale: String[0..1];
}

// One named input a model will not run without: a curve, a surface, a correlation matrix, a
// dividend forecast or a scalar. Exists as data rather than as documentation because the
// missing-input case is the most common production failure and it has to be queryable.
Class product_pricing::PprModelInput
{
   // Which model needs it.
   modelCode: String[1];
   // The input's code within that model, e.g. DISCOUNT_CURVE, VOL_SURFACE, CORR_MATRIX.
   inputCode: String[1];
   // What it is called in the model documentation.
   inputName: String[0..1];
   // CURVE, SURFACE, CORRELATION, DIVIDEND or SCALAR.
   inputKind: String[0..1];
   // True when the model cannot produce a number without it. A false here plus a missing
   // value is a degraded price, not a failed one, and the two must be told apart.
   isMandatory: Boolean[1];
   // Where the value is expected to come from.
   sourceReference: String[0..1];
   // The value used when the input is optional and absent.
   defaultValue: Float[0..1];
   // PERCENT, BASIS_POINTS, YEARS or ABSOLUTE -- so a number is never read on the wrong scale.
   unitOfMeasure: String[0..1];
}

// ============================================================ the market inputs

// An implied volatility surface: one underlying, one as-of date, and the whole grid of
// expiry against strike. Exists as its own class because every model that needs volatility
// needs THIS object, not a number, and because arbitrage-freedom is a property of the
// surface as a whole and cannot be stated on a point.
Class product_pricing::PprVolatilitySurface
{
   // The surface identifier a product or a calibration points at.
   surfaceCode: String[1];
   // The underlying it describes.
   underlyingCode: String[0..1];
   // EQUITY, FX, RATES or COMMODITY.
   surfaceType: String[0..1];
   // LOG_MONEYNESS, DELTA or ABSOLUTE_STRIKE -- the axis the quotes are stated on.
   quoteConvention: String[0..1];
   // The date the surface was built for.
   asOfDate: StrictDate[0..1];
   // At-the-money volatility at the benchmark expiry -- the one number people quote.
   atmVolatility: Float[0..1];
   // The 25-delta risk reversal: how asymmetric the surface is, which is what the ATM
   // number hides and what a barrier product is actually exposed to.
   skew25Delta: Float[0..1];
   // True when the surface passes calendar and butterfly arbitrage checks. A surface that
   // fails these will produce negative densities and prices nobody can explain.
   isArbitrageFree: Boolean[1];
   // LINEAR, CUBIC_SPLINE or SVI -- how the gaps between quotes are filled.
   interpolation: String[0..1];
   // How many quoted points went into it.
   pointCount: Integer[0..1];

   // The points themselves.
   points: product_pricing::PprVolatilityPoint[*];
}

// One quoted point on a volatility surface: an expiry, a strike bucket and the implied
// volatility there. Separate from the surface because a point can be stale, interpolated or
// quoted by nobody, and a surface-level flag cannot say which point that was.
Class product_pricing::PprVolatilityPoint
{
   // The surface this point belongs to.
   surfaceCode: String[1];
   // The expiry pillar, e.g. 1M, 3M, 1Y.
   expiryTenor: String[1];
   // The strike bucket, e.g. 80, 90, ATM, 110, 25DP, 25DC.
   strikeBucket: String[1];
   // The bucket as a percentage of spot, for arithmetic rather than for display.
   strikePercent: Float[0..1];
   // The mid implied volatility at that point.
   impliedVolatility: Float[0..1];
   // The bid side of the volatility quote.
   bidVolatility: Float[0..1];
   // The ask side of it. The spread between the two is the honest width of the mark.
   askVolatility: Float[0..1];
   // True when the point was interpolated rather than quoted. An interpolated point that a
   // barrier sits exactly on is a model reserve waiting to be sized.
   isInterpolated: Boolean[1];
   // How many market quotes backed it.
   quoteCount: Integer[0..1];

   // The width of the volatility market at this point, in volatility units.
   volatilitySpread() { $this.askVolatility->toOne() - $this.bidVolatility->toOne() } : Float[1];
}

// A discount curve: the term structure everything in this project is discounted on.
// Exists as its own class because WHICH curve is the largest single valuation choice after
// the model itself -- a collateralised trade and an uncollateralised one discount
// differently and are otherwise identical.
Class product_pricing::PprDiscountCurve
{
   // The curve identifier a product or a calibration points at.
   curveCode: String[1];
   // What it is called on a screen.
   curveName: String[0..1];
   // ISO 4217 the curve is in.
   currencyCode: String[0..1];
   // OIS, IBOR, FUNDING or GOVERNMENT.
   curveType: String[0..1];
   // The date it was built for.
   asOfDate: StrictDate[0..1];
   // LINEAR_ZERO, LOG_LINEAR_DF or CUBIC_SPLINE.
   interpolation: String[0..1];
   // True for a collateral discount curve. The flag that decides whether this curve may be
   // used for a CSA trade at all.
   isCollateralised: Boolean[1];
   // ISO 4217 of the collateral the curve assumes, when it assumes any.
   collateralCurrency: String[0..1];
   // How many pillars were solved.
   nodeCount: Integer[0..1];

   // The pillars.
   points: product_pricing::PprCurvePoint[*];
}

// One pillar on a discount curve: a tenor, the zero rate solved there and the discount
// factor it implies. Separate from the curve because a pillar is either a market
// instrument or an interpolation, and only the pillar knows which it is.
Class product_pricing::PprCurvePoint
{
   // Which curve.
   curveCode: String[1];
   // The tenor pillar, e.g. 1M, 6M, 2Y, 10Y.
   tenorCode: String[1];
   // The same tenor in days, for arithmetic.
   tenorDays: Integer[0..1];
   // The continuously compounded zero rate solved at this pillar.
   zeroRate: Float[0..1];
   // The discount factor it implies. Carried as well as the rate because rounding one and
   // re-deriving the other is how two systems come to disagree about the same curve.
   discountFactor: Float[0..1];
   // The forward rate over the segment ending here.
   forwardRate: Float[0..1];
   // True when a market instrument was solved to this pillar rather than interpolated to it.
   isPillar: Boolean[1];

   // The tenor in years, which is what every formula in this project actually wants.
   tenorYears() { $this.tenorDays->toOne()->toFloat() / 365.0 } : Float[1];
}

// One correlation between two underlyings in a named matrix. Exists as its own class
// because correlation is the input nobody can hedge and nobody can observe directly, which
// makes it the input that most model reserves are actually held against.
Class product_pricing::PprCorrelationEntry
{
   // The matrix this entry belongs to.
   matrixCode: String[1];
   // The first underlying, by code.
   underlyingOne: String[1];
   // The second. The pair is ordered so the matrix is stored once, not twice.
   underlyingTwo: String[1];
   // The correlation itself, in [-1, 1].
   correlationValue: Float[0..1];
   // How many business days of history it was estimated from.
   estimationWindowDays: Integer[0..1];
   // HISTORICAL, IMPLIED or EXPERT_JUDGEMENT. The third is a euphemism and is why the
   // method is stored rather than assumed.
   estimationMethod: String[0..1];
   // True when this is the stressed value used for reserve sizing rather than the base one.
   isStressed: Boolean[1];
   // The date it was estimated on.
   asOfDate: StrictDate[0..1];
}

// A forecast dividend on an underlying: an ex-date and an amount. Exists as data because
// on a lattice a dividend has a DATE, and turning it into a continuous yield is precisely
// the approximation that gets American call early exercise wrong.
Class product_pricing::PprDividendForecast
{
   // The underlying paying it.
   underlyingCode: String[1];
   // The ex-date. On a lattice this lands on a node; in a yield it disappears.
   exDate: StrictDate[1];
   // The amount per share.
   dividendAmount: Float[0..1];
   // ISO 4217 the amount is in.
   dividendCurrency: String[0..1];
   // True when the amount is a forecast rather than a declared dividend.
   isForecast: Boolean[1];
   // Where the forecast came from.
   forecastSource: String[0..1];
   // The implied annual yield, for the models that want a yield rather than a schedule.
   dividendYield: Float[0..1];
   // True for a special dividend, which no yield model will ever anticipate.
   isSpecial: Boolean[1];
}

// ============================================================ calibration

// One calibration of one model on one date: the fit that turned market quotes into model
// parameters. Exists as its own class because a model price is only meaningful with the
// calibration that produced its parameters, and that fit either converged or it did not.
Class product_pricing::PprCalibration
{
   // The model calibrated.
   modelCode: String[1];
   // The date calibrated for.
   calibrationDate: StrictDate[1];
   // GLOBAL, BOOTSTRAP or LOCAL -- what was actually solved.
   calibrationType: String[0..1];
   // How many instruments were in the target basket.
   targetCount: Integer[0..1];
   // How many solver iterations it took.
   iterationCount: Integer[0..1];
   // Root mean square fit error across the basket, in the quote's own units.
   rootMeanSquareError: Float[0..1];
   // The worst single fit error. A good RMSE with a terrible max error means one product
   // is being priced off the one quote the model cannot fit.
   maxAbsoluteError: Float[0..1];
   // True when the solver converged. A price from a non-converged calibration is not a
   // price, and this flag is the only thing that says so.
   hasConverged: Boolean[1];
   // LEVENBERG_MARQUARDT, NELDER_MEAD or NEWTON.
   solverName: String[0..1];
   // How long it took, in seconds.
   runSeconds: Float[0..1];
   // Who ran it, or which scheduler did.
   calibratedBy: String[0..1];
   // The volatility surface it was calibrated to.
   surfaceCode: String[0..1];
   // The discount curve it was calibrated on.
   curveCode: String[0..1];

   // The basket it was fitted to.
   targets: product_pricing::PprCalibrationTarget[*];
   // The surface it read.
   surface: product_pricing::PprVolatilitySurface[0..1];
   // The curve it discounted on.
   curve: product_pricing::PprDiscountCurve[0..1];

   // Average fit error per target instrument, which is the number a calibration report
   // trends over time. `->toFloat()` because Integer against Float would type as Number.
   errorPerTarget() { $this.rootMeanSquareError->toOne() / $this.targetCount->toOne()->toFloat() } : Float[1];
}

// One instrument in a calibration basket, with the market quote it was fitted to and the
// quote the model returned. Exists per instrument because a fit is only as trustworthy as
// its worst target, and excluding a target is a decision that has to leave a record.
Class product_pricing::PprCalibrationTarget
{
   // The model being calibrated.
   modelCode: String[1];
   // The date being calibrated for.
   calibrationDate: StrictDate[1];
   // Which target this is within the basket.
   targetSeq: Integer[1];
   // What was fitted: an option, a swap, a quoted volatility.
   targetInstrument: String[0..1];
   // The tenor pillar it sits on.
   targetTenor: String[0..1];
   // The market's quote.
   marketQuote: Float[0..1];
   // The model's quote after the fit. The difference is the fit error.
   modelQuote: Float[0..1];
   // The weight this target carried in the objective function.
   targetWeight: Float[0..1];
   // True when it was dropped from the fit.
   isExcluded: Boolean[1];
   // Why it was dropped. An excluded target with no reason is an unexplained fit.
   exclusionReason: String[0..1];

   // The absolute fit error at this target, in the quote's units.
   quoteError() { $this.modelQuote->toOne() - $this.marketQuote->toOne() } : Float[1];
   // The same error as a fraction of the market quote, via valuation-core's deviation
   // function -- the same one a valuation break is measured with.
   quoteErrorPct() { valuation_core::vcDeviationPct($this.marketQuote->toOne(), $this.modelQuote->toOne()) } : Float[1];
}

// ============================================================ what the models produce

// One execution of the pricing process: a batch of products, a cob date, and the
// valuation-core run it feeds. Exists so a price is always attributable to a run, which is
// what makes re-pricing a date safe.
Class product_pricing::PprPricingRun
{
   // The pricing run identifier carried on every price and greek it produced.
   runId: String[1];
   // valuation-core's run this one feeds. The two are separate because a valuation run
   // consumes prices from several sources and this project is only one of them.
   valuationRunId: String[0..1];
   // The close-of-business date priced as of.
   cobDate: StrictDate[0..1];
   // EOD, INTRADAY or ADHOC.
   runType: String[0..1];
   // PENDING, RUNNING, COMPLETE or FAILED.
   status: String[0..1];
   // How many products the run priced.
   productCount: Integer[1];
   // How many it failed to price. A model that fails is not a model that returns zero.
   failedCount: Integer[1];
   // ISO 4217 the run reports in.
   reportingCurrency: String[0..1];
   // When it started, to the second.
   startedAt: DateTime[0..1];
   // True once the run is signed off and may be reported from.
   isSignedOff: Boolean[1];

   // valuation-core's run, across the project boundary.
   valuationRun: valuation_core::VcValuationRun[0..1];
   // Every price the run produced.
   prices: product_pricing::PprProductPrice[*];

   // The share of the run that produced a price. `->toFloat()` on both sides so the
   // division is Float rather than Number.
   pricedPct() { ($this.productCount - $this.failedCount)->toFloat() / $this.productCount->toFloat() } : Float[1];
}

// The price one model produced for one product in one run, next to the market price it is
// checked against. This is the output of the whole project, and the class where the
// cross-project function calls actually happen.
Class product_pricing::PprProductPrice
[
   // A tolerance of zero would make every price a break; a negative one is nonsense.
   toleranceIsPositive: $this.tolerancePct > 0.0,
   // A zero FX rate silently zeroes the reported value, exactly as in valuation-core.
   fxRateIsPositive: $this.fxRate > 0.0
]
{
   // Which pricing run produced it.
   runId: String[1];
   // Which product it is for -- product-core's catalogue key.
   productCode: String[1];
   // The instrument row behind that product, which is how it reaches valuation-core.
   instrumentId: String[1];
   // valuation-core's run this price was fed into.
   valuationRunId: String[0..1];
   // The date priced as of.
   cobDate: StrictDate[0..1];
   // The model that produced it.
   modelCode: String[0..1];
   // The model's clean price, EXCLUDING accrued -- the same convention valuation-core uses,
   // deliberately, so the two numbers can be compared without a translation step.
   cleanModelPrice: Float[1];
   // Interest accrued since the last coupon, per unit. Zero for anything without a coupon.
   accruedInterest: Float[1];
   // How many units the price applies to.
   quantity: Float[1];
   // The observable market price, where there is one. This is what the model is checked
   // against, and its absence is why some products carry a reserve.
   marketPrice: Float[1];
   // The rate carrying the value into the reporting currency, already pointing the right
   // way, as valuation-core stores it.
   fxRate: Float[1];
   // ISO 4217 the model priced in.
   pricingCurrency: String[0..1];
   // ISO 4217 the firm reports in.
   reportingCurrency: String[0..1];
   // The model's own confidence in the number, as a percentage. Only meaningful for the
   // simulated models, which is why it is optional.
   confidencePercent: Float[0..1];
   // The deviation from market that raises a break for this product.
   tolerancePct: Float[1];
   // True when the reported number is the model's rather than the market's.
   isModelPrice: Boolean[1];
   // PRICED, FAILED, STALE or OVERRIDDEN.
   priceStatus: String[0..1];

   // valuation-core's valuation this price fed, on its full three-column key.
   valuation: valuation_core::VcValuation[0..1];
   // The model that produced it.
   model: product_pricing::PprPricingModel[0..1];
   // The first-order greek block computed alongside it.
   greekProfile: product_pricing::PprGreekProfile[0..1];
   // Every individual greek, including the ones outside the standard block.
   greeks: product_pricing::PprGreekObservation[*];
   // Every reserve held against it.
   reserves: product_pricing::PprModelReserve[*];

   // ---- THE CROSS-PROJECT CALLS ---------------------------------------------------------
   // The dirty model price. Not written here: valuation-core owns the definition of what a
   // dirty price is, and a model price that computed it differently would be incomparable
   // with the market price beside it.
   dirtyModelPrice() { valuation_core::vcDirtyPrice($this.cleanModelPrice, $this.accruedInterest) } : Float[1];
   // Model value in the pricing currency, through valuation-core's own multiply.
   modelValue() { valuation_core::vcMarketValue($this.quantity, valuation_core::vcDirtyPrice($this.cleanModelPrice, $this.accruedInterest)) } : Float[1];
   // Model value in the reporting currency. valuation-core delegates the FX leg to core-fx,
   // so this one property reaches three projects down and multiplies nothing itself.
   modelValueInReporting() { valuation_core::vcMarketValueInReporting($this.quantity, valuation_core::vcDirtyPrice($this.cleanModelPrice, $this.accruedInterest), $this.fxRate) } : Float[1];
   // How far the model is from the market, as a fraction of the market price -- measured
   // with the same function a valuation break is measured with.
   modelVsMarketPct() { valuation_core::vcDeviationPct($this.marketPrice, $this.cleanModelPrice) } : Float[1];
   // Whether that deviation is inside this product's tolerance. False is a break.
   isWithinTolerance() { product_pricing::pprIsWithinTolerance(valuation_core::vcDeviationPct($this.marketPrice, $this.cleanModelPrice), $this.tolerancePct) } : Boolean[1];
}

// The standard first-order greek block for one price: delta, gamma, vega, theta, rho, and
// how they were produced. Exists as one class rather than five rows because the block is
// computed together, from one model state, and mixing greeks from two states is wrong in a
// way no per-row flag would catch.
Class product_pricing::PprGreekProfile
{
   // The run the greeks belong to.
   runId: String[1];
   // The product they are for.
   productCode: String[1];
   // Sensitivity of price to a one-unit move in the underlying.
   delta: Float[1];
   // Sensitivity of DELTA to that move -- the reason a delta hedge has to be re-struck.
   gamma: Float[1];
   // Sensitivity of price to a one-point move in volatility. Zero for everything in the
   // accrual and NAV families, which is the honest answer and not a missing number.
   vega: Float[1];
   // Sensitivity of price to the passage of one year.
   theta: Float[1];
   // Sensitivity of price to a parallel move in rates.
   rho: Float[1];
   // The underlying level the block was computed at. Without it the cash greeks below
   // cannot be reconstructed and the block is not reproducible.
   spotLevel: Float[1];
   // The position size the cash greeks are scaled by.
   quantity: Float[1];
   // ANALYTIC, BUMP or PATHWISE -- how the numbers were obtained. A bumped greek from a
   // Monte Carlo model carries the simulation noise twice.
   greekMethod: String[0..1];
   // The bump size used, in basis points, when the method was BUMP.
   bumpSizeBasisPoints: Float[0..1];
   // True when every greek in the block was produced. A partial block is not a block.
   isComplete: Boolean[1];

   // Cash delta: what a 1% move in the underlying costs the position, in money.
   deltaCash() { product_pricing::pprDeltaCash($this.delta, $this.spotLevel, $this.quantity) } : Float[1];
   // Cash gamma: the second-order term over the same move.
   gammaCash() { product_pricing::pprGammaCash($this.gamma, $this.spotLevel, $this.quantity) } : Float[1];
   // What one volatility point is worth across the whole position, in money.
   vegaCash() { product_pricing::pprVegaForShift($this.vega, 1.0) * $this.quantity } : Float[1];
   // Theta as a daily number, which is how a desk carries it.
   thetaPerDay() { product_pricing::pprThetaPerDay($this.theta) } : Float[1];
}

// One named greek that is not in the standard block: vanna, volga, a bucketed vega, a
// cross-gamma. Exists as its own class because the exotic sensitivities differ by product
// and cannot be columns -- an autocall has a dividend sensitivity a deposit does not.
Class product_pricing::PprGreekObservation
{
   // The run.
   runId: String[1];
   // The product.
   productCode: String[1];
   // The greek's code, e.g. VANNA, VOLGA, CROSS_GAMMA, DIV_RHO, CORR_SENS.
   greekCode: String[1];
   // What it is called in a risk report.
   greekName: String[0..1];
   // 1 for a first derivative, 2 for a second, 3 beyond. The order is what decides whether
   // a bumped estimate is stable enough to hedge on.
   greekOrder: Integer[0..1];
   // The value itself.
   greekValue: Float[0..1];
   // PER_PERCENT, PER_BASIS_POINT or ABSOLUTE.
   greekUnit: String[0..1];
   // The shock size the sensitivity was estimated over.
   shockSize: Float[0..1];
   // True when the sensitivity is a cross term between two risk factors -- the ones that
   // vanish from a report that only ever bumps one factor at a time.
   isCrossGreek: Boolean[1];
   // True when the number is stable across re-runs. An unstable second-order greek from a
   // simulation is noise with a name.
   isStable: Boolean[1];

   // Whether this is a first-order sensitivity, which is the cut a risk report is made on.
   isFirstOrder() { $this.greekOrder->toOne() == 1 } : Boolean[1];
}

// ============================================================ model risk

// A reserve held against a price because the model that produced it is not trusted to the
// last basis point. Exists as its own class, keyed by run, product and reserve type,
// because reserves STACK -- model uncertainty, an unhedgeable parameter and a concentrated
// position are three separate admissions and the firm has to be able to release them
// separately.
Class product_pricing::PprModelReserve
{
   // The pricing run the reserve was computed in.
   runId: String[1];
   // The product it is held against.
   productCode: String[1];
   // MODEL_UNCERTAINTY, PARAMETER_UNCERTAINTY, CONCENTRATION, CLOSE_OUT or DAY_ONE.
   reserveType: String[1];
   // The instrument row, so the reserve can reach valuation-core's adjustment.
   instrumentId: String[0..1];
   // valuation-core's run the matching adjustment was booked in.
   valuationRunId: String[0..1];
   // Which of valuation-core's stacked adjustments this reserve became.
   adjustmentSeq: Integer[0..1];
   // The reserve as a rate, in basis points of base value.
   reserveBasisPoints: Float[1];
   // The reserve in money, in the pricing currency.
   reserveAmount: Float[1];
   // The model value the reserve is a percentage of.
   baseValue: Float[1];
   // The rate carrying the reserve into the reporting currency.
   fxRate: Float[1];
   // How it was sized: DISPERSION, PARAMETER_RANGE, EXPERT_JUDGEMENT, REGULATORY_FLOOR.
   methodology: String[0..1];
   // Who approved holding it.
   approvedBy: String[0..1];
   // True once it has been released back to P&L, which is a decision and not an accident.
   isReleased: Boolean[1];
   // Why it is held. The sentence a regulator reads.
   rationale: String[0..1];

   // valuation-core's mark adjustment this reserve was booked as, across the boundary --
   // the reserve is computed here and lands there.
   markAdjustment: valuation_core::VcMarkAdjustment[0..1];

   // The reserve re-derived from the rate and the base value. Where this disagrees with
   // reserveAmount, one of the two was computed against a different base.
   derivedReserveAmount() { product_pricing::pprReserveAmount($this.baseValue, $this.reserveBasisPoints) } : Float[1];
   // The value actually reported: the model's number, less the admission.
   valueNetOfReserve() { product_pricing::pprNetOfReserve($this.baseValue, $this.reserveAmount) } : Float[1];
}

// One model validation review: who looked at the model, when, and what they concluded.
// Exists as history rather than as a status flag on the model, because "when was it last
// validated" and "what did they find" are the two questions a model risk report is, and a
// flag answers neither.
Class product_pricing::PprModelValidation
{
   // The model reviewed.
   modelCode: String[1];
   // The date of the review.
   reviewDate: StrictDate[1];
   // Who performed it -- independent of the quant team that owns the model.
   validator: String[0..1];
   // PASS, PASS_WITH_CONDITIONS, FAIL or DEFERRED.
   outcome: String[0..1];
   // How many findings were raised.
   findingsCount: Integer[0..1];
   // The severity of the worst one, 1 to 5.
   severity: Integer[0..1];
   // When the model must be looked at again.
   nextReviewDate: StrictDate[0..1];
   // True when approval depends on conditions being met.
   isConditional: Boolean[1];
   // The conditions themselves.
   conditions: String[0..1];

   // Whether the review cleared the model outright.
   isClearPass() { $this.outcome->toOne() == 'PASS' } : Boolean[1];
}

// A known limitation of a model, recorded against it. Exists because a model reserve
// without a stated limitation is an unexplained number, and because the limitation is what
// tells a desk which trades to stop doing rather than which reserve to hold.
Class product_pricing::PprModelLimitation
{
   // The model it applies to.
   modelCode: String[1];
   // The limitation's code, e.g. NO_STOCHASTIC_VOL, FLAT_CORRELATION, NO_JUMPS.
   limitationCode: String[1];
   // What it is, in a sentence.
   limitationDescription: String[0..1];
   // Where it bites: PRICE, DELTA, VEGA, TAIL_RISK, LONG_DATED.
   impactArea: String[0..1];
   // HIGH, MEDIUM or LOW.
   materiality: String[0..1];
   // The PprModelReserve type held because of it, linking the admission to the money.
   reserveType: String[0..1];
   // True when something has been done about it -- a reserve, a scope restriction, a
   // second model run alongside.
   isMitigated: Boolean[1];
   // What was done.
   mitigation: String[0..1];
   // When it was first recorded.
   raisedOn: StrictDate[0..1];
}

// ============================================================ exported functions
// The pricing arithmetic downstream projects call rather than re-derive. Four of them are
// thin wrappers over valuation-core, which is the cross-project call this graph is built to
// exercise: a model price and a market price must travel through the same arithmetic or
// the deviation between them measures the arithmetic and not the model.

// The dirty model price -- clean plus accrued, by valuation-core's definition and not this
// project's. One line, and it is here so that no class in this package writes the addition.
function product_pricing::pprDirtyModelPrice(cleanModelPrice: Float[1], accruedInterest: Float[1]): Float[1]
{
   valuation_core::vcDirtyPrice($cleanModelPrice, $accruedInterest)
}

// Model value in the pricing currency: units times what one unit is modelled to settle at.
function product_pricing::pprModelValue(quantity: Float[1], dirtyModelPrice: Float[1]): Float[1]
{
   valuation_core::vcMarketValue($quantity, $dirtyModelPrice)
}

// Model value in the reporting currency. The FX leg belongs to core-fx, which valuation-core
// already calls, so this project reaches it without naming it.
function product_pricing::pprModelValueInReporting(quantity: Float[1], dirtyModelPrice: Float[1], fxRate: Float[1]): Float[1]
{
   valuation_core::vcMarketValueInReporting($quantity, $dirtyModelPrice, $fxRate)
}

// Model against market, as a fraction of the market price. The same deviation function a
// valuation break is measured with, so a pricing break and a valuation break are the same
// number computed once.
function product_pricing::pprModelVsMarketPct(marketPrice: Float[1], modelPrice: Float[1]): Float[1]
{
   valuation_core::vcDeviationPct($marketPrice, $modelPrice)
}

// Whether a deviation is inside a tolerance. Signed deviation, unsigned test: a model 3%
// cheap is as wrong as a model 3% rich.
function product_pricing::pprIsWithinTolerance(deviationPct: Float[1], tolerancePct: Float[1]): Boolean[1]
{
   $deviationPct->abs() <= $tolerancePct
}

// A reserve in money, from a reserve in basis points. The 10000 appears here once, so no
// class in this package divides by it.
function product_pricing::pprReserveAmount(baseValue: Float[1], reserveBasisPoints: Float[1]): Float[1]
{
   $baseValue * ($reserveBasisPoints / 10000.0)
}

// The value actually reported, after the reserve is taken off the model's number.
function product_pricing::pprNetOfReserve(baseValue: Float[1], reserveAmount: Float[1]): Float[1]
{
   $baseValue - $reserveAmount
}

// Cash delta: the money at risk in a one-unit move of the underlying, for a position.
function product_pricing::pprDeltaCash(delta: Float[1], spotLevel: Float[1], quantity: Float[1]): Float[1]
{
   $delta * $spotLevel * $quantity
}

// Cash gamma: the second-order term of the same move, with the half that a Taylor
// expansion requires and that hand-written versions of this leave out.
function product_pricing::pprGammaCash(gamma: Float[1], spotLevel: Float[1], quantity: Float[1]): Float[1]
{
   0.5 * $gamma * $spotLevel * $spotLevel * $quantity
}

// What a stated volatility shift is worth, given a vega. Stated as a function so the scale
// of the shift is the caller's problem exactly once.
function product_pricing::pprVegaForShift(vega: Float[1], volShiftPoints: Float[1]): Float[1]
{
   $vega * $volShiftPoints
}

// Theta per calendar day, from theta per year. 365 and not 252: theta is carry, and carry
// accrues on weekends.
function product_pricing::pprThetaPerDay(thetaPerYear: Float[1]): Float[1]
{
   $thetaPerYear / 365.0
}

// The size of one lattice step, in years. `->toFloat()` on the step count because Integer
// against Float types as Number, which is not a subtype of Float.
function product_pricing::pprLatticeStepSize(yearsToExpiry: Float[1], stepCount: Integer[1]): Float[1]
{
   $yearsToExpiry / $stepCount->toFloat()
}

// The Monte Carlo standard error: the path standard deviation over the square root of the
// path count. The whole economics of simulation is in that square root -- four times the
// paths for half the error -- and it is written once, here.
function product_pricing::pprMonteCarloStandardError(pathStandardDeviation: Float[1], pathCount: Integer[1]): Float[1]
{
   $pathStandardDeviation / sqrt($pathCount->toFloat())
}

// Whether a simulated price has converged to its stated target, in basis points of price.
function product_pricing::pprHasConverged(standardError: Float[1], targetBasisPoints: Float[1]): Boolean[1]
{
   $standardError <= ($targetBasisPoints / 10000.0)
}
